The vendored modules carry a `Vendored from openfox-forge@<sha>` header naming
the upstream revision they came from. The resynchronisation procedure is
runnable:

    python scripts/check_vendored_sync.py [path-to-openfox-forge]

It diffs every `launcher/*.py` against upstream and classifies each changed
line, so the intended differences are visible instead of asserted:

* the **provenance header**;
* the **product rename** this repository forces — `openfox-forge` →
  `minimax-launcher`, the `OPENFOX_FORGE_*` / `FORGE_*` environment variables
  → `MINIMAX_LAUNCHER_*` / `LAUNCHER_*`, the product name, the bundled asset
  names. It is required by the rule that no agent-surface name survives in
  `launcher/`, and it is why the logger namespaces differ;
* the **removal of the text-agent surface** — the `agent` and `llamacpp`
  stacks, the MCP tool layer, `openfox.py` / `agent_cli.py` / `searxng.py` /
  `deepseek.py`, the LLM profiles and the llama.cpp registry;
* the **derived work** done here and not upstream: the cross-platform
  backends (Keychain, Secret Service, `lsof`/`ss`, `osascript`/`notify-send`),
  the public-template default, the first-run wizard and the single-artefact
  release build.

Modules that carry none of the last two differ by the header and the rename
alone.

The image, the pod-side scripts (`install.sh`, `lib/`, `Dockerfile`,
`workflows/`, `presets/`) remain this repository's own work under Apache-2.0.
